WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.896
Welcome to Lecture 10.

00:00:01.896 --> 00:00:09.080
Last lecture, a dense network learned features by connecting all input coordinates to hidden units.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:13.217
For a 32 by 32 color image, that means 3072 input values.

00:00:13.217 --> 00:00:19.022
Convolution keeps the image's spatial arrangement and uses small shared filters.

00:00:19.022 --> 00:00:31.140
Our goal is to understand what those filters compute, how the output dimensions are determined, and how convolution, activation, pooling and dense layers fit together.

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:36.125
The lecture's history begins with adaptive linear models around 1960

00:00:36.125 --> 00:00:39.942
and the later popularization of backpropagation in 1986.

00:00:39.942 --> 00:00:46.077
The early convolutional document-recognition network demonstrated gradient-based learning,

00:00:46.077 --> 00:00:48.190
with the influential 1998 paper

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:52.962
connecting convolutional feature maps and subsampling to a classifier.

00:00:52.962 --> 00:00:57.869
These historical examples show that convolution is an architectural idea

00:00:57.869 --> 00:00:59.505
with a long development,

00:00:59.505 --> 00:01:04.686
rather than a technique that appeared suddenly with today's language models.

00:01:04.686 --> 00:01:09.730
The dates identify the slide's milestones, not exclusive invention claims.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:20.655
The 2006 deep-learning revival and AlexNet's 2012 ImageNet result helped demonstrate the potential of learned hierarchical representations at larger scale.

00:01:20.655 --> 00:01:26.317
Data, computational resources and training methods mattered alongside architecture.

00:01:26.317 --> 00:01:31.229
Do not infer that every deeper network automatically generalizes better.

00:01:31.229 --> 00:01:34.435
Instead, ask what structure the model exploits.

00:01:34.435 --> 00:01:42.008
Images have local patterns that recur at many positions, making locality and shared weights useful assumptions.

00:01:42.008 --> 00:01:48.080
This is the thread connecting the historical examples to the calculation we will perform.

00:01:48.867 --> 00:01:53.373
The application gallery begins with classification and retrieval.

00:01:53.373 --> 00:01:57.394
Classification might identify the main object in an image.

00:01:57.394 --> 00:02:02.385
Retrieval maps an image to a representation used to find related images.

00:02:02.385 --> 00:02:05.088
These are related but distinct outputs.

00:02:05.088 --> 00:02:08.970
Detection must locate objects and assign labels to them.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:12.714
Segmentation produces a label at each pixel or region.

00:02:12.714 --> 00:02:22.627
A backbone that extracts features can support several tasks, but the final prediction head and training loss must match what the task requires.

00:02:22.977 --> 00:02:26.972
The next examples include driving scenes, video processing

00:02:26.972 --> 00:02:29.726
and estimation of human joint locations.

00:02:29.726 --> 00:02:34.134
Video brings a time dimension in addition to spatial dimensions.

00:02:34.134 --> 00:02:38.129
A pose system predicts structured locations such as joints

00:02:38.129 --> 00:02:40.539
rather than only a single category.

00:02:40.539 --> 00:02:43.638
The hardware picture in the historical slides

00:02:43.638 --> 00:02:47.358
illustrates the importance of accelerated computation;

00:02:47.358 --> 00:02:51.283
specific product names are examples from those materials.

00:02:51.283 --> 00:02:56.449
We do not need that exact hardware to understand the mathematical operation

00:02:56.449 --> 00:02:58.377
or reproduce a tiny example.

00:02:58.727 --> 00:03:05.606
The gallery also includes medical and scientific imagery, traffic signs, galaxies, whales and aerial images.

00:03:05.606 --> 00:03:12.550
Image captioning turns an image representation into a sequence of words, connecting this lecture to the next.

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:19.748
Its illustrated captions include mistakes, reminding us that plausible predictions can still misdescribe a scene.

00:03:19.748 --> 00:03:24.398
Visualization and synthesis examples show other uses of learned features.

00:03:24.398 --> 00:03:35.927
Across all these applications, distinguish the shared feature extractor from the task-specific output, and evaluate predictions against the actual task rather than appearance alone.

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:43.925
The preview diagrams describe a convolutional network as a sequence of three-dimensional activation volumes.

00:03:43.925 --> 00:03:49.344
Width and height describe spatial positions; the third dimension counts channels.

00:03:49.344 --> 00:03:52.622
For an RGB image, there are three input channels.

00:03:52.622 --> 00:03:58.242
Later channels are learned feature maps and do not necessarily correspond to colors.

00:03:58.242 --> 00:04:01.788
Convolution changes the volume using learned filters.

00:04:01.788 --> 00:04:06.805
An elementwise ReLU changes values but leaves the tensor's shape unchanged.

00:04:06.805 --> 00:04:11.220
Pooling can reduce spatial dimensions without mixing the channels.

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:14.082
Follow the first preview example.

00:04:14.082 --> 00:04:17.736
Start with a 32 by 32 image with three channels.

00:04:17.736 --> 00:04:27.784
Six filters, each 5 by 5 across all three input channels, with stride 1 and no padding, produce a 28 by 28 output with six channels.

00:04:27.784 --> 00:04:31.362
Each filter contributes one channel, not three.

00:04:31.362 --> 00:04:35.625
A second layer must therefore accept six input channels.

00:04:35.625 --> 00:04:41.410
Its filters have a different depth even if their spatial width remains five.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:46.369
10 filters in that second layer produce 24 by 24 by 10 activations.

00:04:46.369 --> 00:04:51.116
You can see the spatial shrinking before we derive a general formula.

00:04:51.116 --> 00:04:55.863
Stacking such layers grows the receptive field in the original image.

00:04:55.863 --> 00:05:01.711
The feature-map visualizations in the slides relate one filter to one activation map.

00:05:01.711 --> 00:05:09.553
They are illustrations of learned responses, not proof that every filter has a single human-interpretable meaning.

00:05:09.553 --> 00:05:17.120
Track the dimensions at every arrow, because a mismatched channel count makes a network specification invalid.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:26.471
A convolutional filter spans a small spatial window and, for the ordinary dense convolution considered here, all input channels.

00:05:26.471 --> 00:05:34.036
At one position, multiply each patch value by its corresponding filter weight, add the products and add one bias.

00:05:34.036 --> 00:05:35.710
The result is one number.

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:41.669
A 5 by 5 filter on a three-channel image therefore performs a 75-dimensional dot product.

00:05:41.669 --> 00:05:48.900
It does not produce a separate output number for each input channel; those channel contributions are summed.

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:55.679
Move the filter to the next position and repeat the same calculation with the same learned numbers.

00:05:55.679 --> 00:06:01.069
The input patch changes, but the filter does not gain new parameters when it moves.

00:06:01.069 --> 00:06:03.277
This is spatial parameter sharing.

00:06:03.277 --> 00:06:09.122
The progressive diagrams in the lecture trace several outputs becoming one activation map.

00:06:09.122 --> 00:06:14.642
CNN libraries normally implement cross-correlation, where the kernel is not reversed.

00:06:14.642 --> 00:06:21.850
We retain the conventional name convolution while being explicit about the operation in our executable example.

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:27.319
A second filter has its own weights and produces another activation map.

00:06:27.319 --> 00:06:31.158
Stack all the maps along the output channel dimension.

00:06:31.158 --> 00:06:35.139
Six filters produce six channels; 10 filters produce 10.

00:06:35.139 --> 00:06:43.031
A conventional filter's channel depth equals the current input depth, not the number of filters we have chosen.

00:06:43.031 --> 00:06:51.918
Depthwise and grouped convolutions are useful exceptions with different connectivity, beyond the basic layer in these slides.

00:06:51.918 --> 00:06:59.240
The general principle remains that output shape and learned-parameter count answer different questions.

00:07:00.034 --> 00:07:07.796
Pause and predict the output channel count for an RGB input processed by six ordinary convolutional filters.

00:07:07.796 --> 00:07:09.018
Is it 3, 6 or 18?

00:07:09.018 --> 00:07:13.834
Explain exactly where the input-channel contributions are combined.

00:07:13.834 --> 00:07:21.166
Then consider the next layer: if it uses 10 filters, what input depth must each of those filters span?

00:07:21.166 --> 00:07:28.354
Use the volume diagram rather than multiplying every number you see in the architecture description.

00:07:28.904 --> 00:07:30.909
The first output depth is six.

00:07:30.909 --> 00:07:38.061
Each filter sums across its three input channels at a spatial location and produces one output value there.

00:07:38.061 --> 00:07:40.668
Six filters therefore produce six maps.

00:07:40.668 --> 00:07:46.550
The next layer's filters must each span the six channels arriving from that first layer.

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:49.224
10 such filters produce 10 new channels.

00:07:49.224 --> 00:07:59.384
Your exercise is to label every filter's full three-dimensional shape in the two-layer preview, including the channel depth as well as its spatial size.

00:07:59.734 --> 00:08:01.897
Three ideas summarize this part.

00:08:01.897 --> 00:08:06.561
Convolution applies local dot products to preserve spatial structure.

00:08:06.561 --> 00:08:09.941
One filter reuses its parameters across locations.

00:08:09.941 --> 00:08:13.794
A bank of filters produces a bank of output feature maps.

00:08:13.794 --> 00:08:18.999
The application gallery showed why the final output also depends on the task.

00:08:18.999 --> 00:08:28.734
Next, we derive the spatial formula, investigate strides and padding, and reproduce the lecture's example with exactly 760 trainable parameters.

